<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Related Works</h2>

<div id="Ax1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph-based Model Extraction Attacks &amp; Defenses.</span>
Model Extraction Attacks (MEA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> is a prevalent threat faced by GMLaaS. Due to the unique data structures inherent in graph machine learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stamile et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, existing MEA methods cannot be directly applied to graph models. Consequently, research on graph-based MEA remains in the early stages. The first paper about graph-based MEA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> introduced a taxonomy comprising seven distinct attack scenarios, categorized by the extent of knowledge accessible to the attacker. Specifically, the availability or absence of knowledge regarding node attributes, graph structural information, and partial training data significantly impacts the performance of the surrogate model. Another work, GNNStealing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, proposed a practical inductive graph stealing framework, categorizing attacks into two main types: Type I, in which attackers have knowledge of the graph structure of query data, and Type II, where such structural information is unavailable. Additionally, this work restricted the model outputs to align more closely with realistic scenarios, considering outputs such as node embeddings, soft labels, or node embeddings after dimensionality reduction via t-SNE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van der Maaten and Hinton, 2008</a>)</cite>.
Simultaneously, numerous methods have been proposed to defend against MEA. Among these, one early work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2021</a>)</cite> introduced the idea of embedding watermarks into the target model using random graphs and specific outputs. Ownership can then be claimed if these watermarks are identified in a suspicious model. Furthermore, fingerprinting techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Waheed et al., 2023</a>)</cite> determine model ownership by comparing the distributional proximity of suspicious embeddings with those of independent and target embeddings. In contrast to prior work, watermarking techniques require specific input-output pairs, limiting their applicability to the label level. Similarly, fingerprinting techniques rely on embedding distributions for verification, restricting their use to the embedding level. The CITED framework, however, introduces a unified approach that enables ownership verification across all output levels.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Watermarking &amp; Fingerprinting for GNNs.</span>
Watermarking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boenisch, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Quiring et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh and Singh, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2020b</a>)</cite> is a widely adopted technique to verify intellectual property (IP) ownership. In defending against MEA, task-specific watermarking approaches have been proposed for various graph-learning tasks. For the node classification task, the aforementioned work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2021</a>)</cite> embeds watermarks by incorporating random graphs and assigning specific labels as outputs. For link prediction tasks, GENIE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bachina et al., 2024</a>)</cite> was proposed, embedding watermarks by modifying node features to follow specific distributions, altering subgraph structures, and producing predefined labels. Similarly, for graph classification tasks, another work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> embeds watermarks by adjusting subgraph structures and generating specific labels. More general watermarking techniques for GNNs have also addressed additional concerns. For instance, one recent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> focuses explicitly on watermark imperceptibility and uniqueness. This approach demonstrates strong performance against evasion attacks, where surrogate models attempt to detect trigger graphs to evade generating desired outputs, thus invalidating ownership verification, as well as fraudulent attacks, in which adversaries forge watermarks to falsely claim model ownership.
Additionally, fingerprinting-based defense methods have also been explored; for instance, GrOVe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Waheed et al., 2023</a>)</cite> leverages a theorem stating that independently trained models naturally differ in their embedding distributions. By comparing whether the suspicious model’s embedding distribution more closely aligns with the target model or an independently trained model, GrOVe determines if the suspicious model has been stolen from the target model. Compared with existing methods, our proposed CITED framework effectively preserves the signature in surrogate models even after MEA.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proof of Theoretical Analysis</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Lemma &amp; Propositions</h3>

<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma A.1</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(MPGNN Perturbation Bound <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liao et al., 2020</a>)</cite>)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of layers and denote the parameter vector of an <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer MPGNN by
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}=\operatorname{vec}\!\bigl(\{{\bm{W}}_{1},{\bm{W}}_{2},\dots,{\bm{W}}_{L}\}\bigr)\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.249em;" width="1.249em"><mi>vec</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}=\operatorname{vec}\!\bigl(\{{\bm{W}}_{1},{\bm{W}}_{2},\dots,{\bm{W}}_{L}\}\bigr)\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where each <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}_{\ell}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times d_{\ell-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}_{\ell}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times d_{\ell-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight matrix of layer <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider an input feature matrix <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{X}}\in\mathbb{R}^{|{\mathcal{V}}|\times d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{X}}\in\mathbb{R}^{|{\mathcal{V}}|\times d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> that lies in
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{R,{\bm{h}}_{0}}:=\{\,{\bm{X}}\mid\|{\bm{X}}-{\bm{h}}_{0}\|_{2}\leq R\,\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{R,{\bm{h}}_{0}}:=\{\,{\bm{X}}\mid\|{\bm{X}}-{\bm{h}}_{0}\|_{2}\leq R\,\},</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{h}}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{|{\mathcal{V}}|\times d_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{|{\mathcal{V}}|\times d_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> a fixed reference and <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> a radius.
Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the adjacency matrix of a graph <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}=({\mathcal{V}},{\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}=({\mathcal{V}},{\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> be its maximum node degree.
For a perturbation
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}=\operatorname{vec}\!\bigl(\{{\bm{U}}_{1},{\bm{U}}_{2},\dots,{\bm{U}}_{L}\}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.249em;" width="1.249em"><mi>vec</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}=\operatorname{vec}\!\bigl(\{{\bm{U}}_{1},{\bm{U}}_{2},\dots,{\bm{U}}_{L}\}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfying
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta:=\max_{\ell\in[L]}\frac{\|{\bm{U}}_{\ell}\|_{2}}{\|{\bm{W}}_{\ell}\|_{2}}\leq\frac{1}{L}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta:=\max_{\ell\in[L]}\frac{\|{\bm{U}}_{\ell}\|_{2}}{\|{\bm{W}}_{\ell}\|_{2}}\leq\frac{1}{L},</annotation></semantics></math>
the output change of the MPGNN obeys</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl\|{\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}+{\bm{u}}}({\bm{X}},{\bm{A}})-{\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}}({\bm{X}},{\bm{A}})\bigr\|_{2}\;\leq\;\mathrm{e}\,R\,L\,\eta\,\|{\bm{W}}_{1}\|_{2}\,\|{\bm{W}}_{L}\|_{2}\,C_{\phi}\,\frac{(dC)^{L-1}-1}{dC-1}\;=\;\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>𝒘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\|{\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}+{\bm{u}}}({\bm{X}},{\bm{A}})-{\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}}({\bm{X}},{\bm{A}})\bigr\|_{2}\;\leq\;\mathrm{e}\,R\,L\,\eta\,\|{\bm{W}}_{1}\|_{2}\,\|{\bm{W}}_{L}\|_{2}\,C_{\phi}\,\frac{(dC)^{L-1}-1}{dC-1}\;=\;\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=C_{\phi}\,C_{\rho}\,C_{g}\,\|{\bm{W}}_{2}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=C_{\phi}\,C_{\rho}\,C_{g}\,\|{\bm{W}}_{2}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\phi},C_{\rho},C_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\phi},C_{\rho},C_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are the Lipschitz constants of the activation function, the message aggregation operator, and the graph normalization operator, respectively.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1 (MPGNN Perturbation Bound ( , )). ‣ A.1 Lemma &amp; Propositions ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theoretical Analysis ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>, we conclude that for any MPGNN whose parameter perturbation <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\leq 1/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\leq 1/L</annotation></semantics></math>, the discrepancy between the perturbed model <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}+{\bm{u}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}+{\bm{u}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the original model <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>𝒘</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is upper bounded by <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This bound depends on the radius <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> of the input set, the perturbation ratio <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, the spectral norms of the first and last weight matrices, the Lipschitz constants of the constituent operators, and the graph degree <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dC\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dC\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> the bound takes the closed form shown above, if <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dC=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dC=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}=\mathrm{e}\,R\,L\,\eta\,\|{\bm{W}}_{1}\|_{2}\,\|{\bm{W}}_{L}\|_{2}\,C_{\phi}\,(L-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}=\mathrm{e}\,R\,L\,\eta\,\|{\bm{W}}_{1}\|_{2}\,\|{\bm{W}}_{L}\|_{2}\,C_{\phi}\,(L-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. A detailed proof is provided in the <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liao et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition A.2</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Embedding Wasserstein Bound)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{e}}={\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}}({\bm{X}},{\bm{A}})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{e}}={\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}}({\bm{X}},{\bm{A}})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\bm{e}}}={\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}+{\bm{u}}}({\bm{X}},{\bm{A}})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\bm{e}}}={\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}+{\bm{u}}}({\bm{X}},{\bm{A}})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>
be the node‑level embeddings produced by the original and the perturbed MPGNN, respectively.
Interpret each embedding as a Dirac measure,
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{{\bm{e}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>𝐞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{{\bm{e}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\tilde{{\bm{e}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\tilde{{\bm{e}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, on <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{p}\!\bigl(\delta_{{\bm{e}}},\delta_{\tilde{{\bm{e}}}}\bigr)\;\leq\;\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>𝒆</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{p}\!\bigl(\delta_{{\bm{e}}},\delta_{\tilde{{\bm{e}}}}\bigr)\;\leq\;\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the perturbation bound established in Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1 (MPGNN Perturbation Bound ( , )). ‣ A.1 Lemma &amp; Propositions ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theoretical Analysis ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">For Dirac measures, the <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-Wasserstein distance collapses to the Euclidean distance between their support points,
that is
<math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{p}(\delta_{{\bm{e}}},\delta_{\tilde{{\bm{e}}}})=\lVert{\bm{e}}-\tilde{{\bm{e}}}\rVert_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>𝒆</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{p}(\delta_{{\bm{e}}},\delta_{\tilde{{\bm{e}}}})=\lVert{\bm{e}}-\tilde{{\bm{e}}}\rVert_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
for every <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1 (MPGNN Perturbation Bound ( , )). ‣ A.1 Lemma &amp; Propositions ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theoretical Analysis ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> guarantees
<math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert{\bm{e}}-\tilde{{\bm{e}}}\rVert_{2}\leq\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert{\bm{e}}-\tilde{{\bm{e}}}\rVert_{2}\leq\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which directly implies the desired inequality.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof for Proposition <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition A.3 (Label Confidence Bound). ‣ A.1 Lemma &amp; Propositions ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theoretical Analysis ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The softmax map <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:\mathbb{R}^{C}\to\Delta^{C-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:\mathbb{R}^{C}\to\Delta^{C-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>‑Lipschitz under the
<math id="A1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm, i.e. <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\sigma(u)-\sigma(v)\rVert_{2}\leq\lVert u-v\rVert_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\sigma(u)-\sigma(v)\rVert_{2}\leq\lVert u-v\rVert_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v</annotation></semantics></math>.
The maximum operator <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm conf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>conf</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm conf}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>‑Lipschitz on the probability simplex
with respect to the <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm as well.
Therefore</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigl\lvert{\rm conf}(p)-{\rm conf}(\tilde{p})\bigr\rvert\leq\lVert p-\tilde{p}\rVert_{2}\leq\lVert z-\tilde{z}\rVert_{2}\leq\mathcal{B}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>conf</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>conf</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\lvert{\rm conf}(p)-{\rm conf}(\tilde{p})\bigr\rvert\leq\lVert p-\tilde{p}\rVert_{2}\leq\lVert z-\tilde{z}\rVert_{2}\leq\mathcal{B},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the last inequality follows from Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1 (MPGNN Perturbation Bound ( , )). ‣ A.1 Lemma &amp; Propositions ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theoretical Analysis ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition A.3</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Label Confidence Bound)<span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}={\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}}({\bm{X}},{\bm{A}})\in\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}={\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}}({\bm{X}},{\bm{A}})\in\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\bm{z}}}={\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}+{\bm{u}}}({\bm{X}},{\bm{A}})\in\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\bm{z}}}={\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}+{\bm{u}}}({\bm{X}},{\bm{A}})\in\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>
be the logit vectors of a graph produced by the original and the perturbed MPGNN, respectively, where <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the hypothesis of Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1 (MPGNN Perturbation Bound ( , )). ‣ A.1 Lemma &amp; Propositions ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theoretical Analysis ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.
Set the soft labels to
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{p}}=\operatorname{softmax}({\bm{z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{p}}=\operatorname{softmax}({\bm{z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\bm{p}}}=\operatorname{softmax}(\tilde{{\bm{z}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\bm{p}}}=\operatorname{softmax}(\tilde{{\bm{z}}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define the prediction confidence as
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{conf}({\bm{p}})=\max_{j}p_{j}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>conf</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{conf}({\bm{p}})=\max_{j}p_{j}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Then, for every graph input covered by Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1 (MPGNN Perturbation Bound ( , )). ‣ A.1 Lemma &amp; Propositions ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theoretical Analysis ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigl\lvert\operatorname{conf}({\bm{p}})-\operatorname{conf}(\tilde{{\bm{p}}})\bigr\rvert\;\leq\;\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>conf</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>conf</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.169em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\lvert\operatorname{conf}({\bm{p}})-\operatorname{conf}(\tilde{{\bm{p}}})\bigr\rvert\;\leq\;\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The soft‑max map <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:\mathbb{R}^{C}\!\to\!\Delta^{C-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:\mathbb{R}^{C}\!\to\!\Delta^{C-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz under the Euclidean norm,
so
<math id="A1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert{\bm{p}}-\tilde{{\bm{p}}}\rVert_{2}\leq\lVert{\bm{z}}-\tilde{{\bm{z}}}\rVert_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert{\bm{p}}-\tilde{{\bm{p}}}\rVert_{2}\leq\lVert{\bm{z}}-\tilde{{\bm{z}}}\rVert_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The maximum operator <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{conf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>conf</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{conf}</annotation></semantics></math> is also <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz on the simplex, hence</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigl\lvert\operatorname{conf}({\bm{p}})-\operatorname{conf}(\tilde{{\bm{p}}})\bigr\rvert\leq\lVert{\bm{p}}-\tilde{{\bm{p}}}\rVert_{2}\leq\lVert{\bm{z}}-\tilde{{\bm{z}}}\rVert_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>conf</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>conf</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl\lvert\operatorname{conf}({\bm{p}})-\operatorname{conf}(\tilde{{\bm{p}}})\bigr\rvert\leq\lVert{\bm{p}}-\tilde{{\bm{p}}}\rVert_{2}\leq\lVert{\bm{z}}-\tilde{{\bm{z}}}\rVert_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1 (MPGNN Perturbation Bound ( , )). ‣ A.1 Lemma &amp; Propositions ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theoretical Analysis ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> bounds the right‑most term by <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, completing the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Proof for Theorem 1</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Probabilistic Wasserstein Bound). ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{p}=W_{p}(\delta_{{\bm{e}}_{a}},\delta_{{\bm{e}}_{b}})=\lVert{\bm{e}}_{a}-{\bm{e}}_{b}\rVert_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{p}=W_{p}(\delta_{{\bm{e}}_{a}},\delta_{{\bm{e}}_{b}})=\lVert{\bm{e}}_{a}-{\bm{e}}_{b}\rVert_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the mean‑value (Fréchet) expansion of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}+{\bm{u}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}+{\bm{u}}}</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{e}}_{b}-{\bm{e}}_{a}=\sum_{k=1}^{L}\Bigl(\tfrac{\partial{\bm{f}}}{\partial{\bm{W}}_{k}}\Bigr){\bm{U}}_{k}\;+\;\mathbf{R}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝒇</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑼</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{e}}_{b}-{\bm{e}}_{a}=\sum_{k=1}^{L}\Bigl(\tfrac{\partial{\bm{f}}}{\partial{\bm{W}}_{k}}\Bigr){\bm{U}}_{k}\;+\;\mathbf{R},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">where
<math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}=O\!\bigl(\lVert{\bm{u}}\rVert_{2}^{2}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.621em;" width="0.621em"><mi>O</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}=O\!\bigl(\lVert{\bm{u}}\rVert_{2}^{2}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> collects second‑order and higher terms.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bounding the remainder.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1 (MPGNN Perturbation Bound ( , )). ‣ A.1 Lemma &amp; Propositions ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theoretical Analysis ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> imposes
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\max_{k}\lVert{\bm{U}}_{k}\rVert_{2}/\lVert{\bm{W}}_{k}\rVert_{2}\leq 1/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mi>𝑼</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\max_{k}\lVert{\bm{U}}_{k}\rVert_{2}/\lVert{\bm{W}}_{k}\rVert_{2}\leq 1/L</annotation></semantics></math>,
so <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert{\bm{u}}\rVert_{2}=O(\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert{\bm{u}}\rVert_{2}=O(\eta)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\mathbf{R}\rVert_{2}=O(\eta^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\mathbf{R}\rVert_{2}=O(\eta^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\leq 1/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\leq 1/L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least one order smaller than the linear term
and can be absorbed into the constant <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math>; thus we focus on the linear part</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}=\sum_{k=1}^{L}\Bigl(\tfrac{\partial{\bm{f}}}{\partial{\bm{W}}_{k}}\Bigr){\bm{U}}_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝒇</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑼</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}=\sum_{k=1}^{L}\Bigl(\tfrac{\partial{\bm{f}}}{\partial{\bm{W}}_{k}}\Bigr){\bm{U}}_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sub‑Gaussian tail for the linear term.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each summand
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\tfrac{\partial{\bm{f}}}{\partial{\bm{W}}_{k}}\bigr){\bm{U}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝒇</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑼</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\tfrac{\partial{\bm{f}}}{\partial{\bm{W}}_{k}}\bigr){\bm{U}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a mean‑zero random vector that is
independent across <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and has norm bounded by
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\,\eta\;\bigl(\prod_{i&lt;k}\lVert{\bm{W}}_{i}\rVert_{2}\bigr)\rho_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\,\eta\;\bigl(\prod_{i&lt;k}\lVert{\bm{W}}_{i}\rVert_{2}\bigr)\rho_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
matching the components used in <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Vector Bernstein (or equivalently the Hanson–Wright inequality applied coordinate‑wise)
therefore gives, for every <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pr\!\bigl(\lVert\mathbf{S}\rVert_{2}\geq t\bigr)\;\leq\;\exp\!\bigl(-t^{2}/(2\sigma^{2})\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.090em;" width="1.090em"><mi>Pr</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝐒</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mi>t</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\!\bigl(\lVert\mathbf{S}\rVert_{2}\geq t\bigr)\;\leq\;\exp\!\bigl(-t^{2}/(2\sigma^{2})\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tail bound for <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\mathbf{R}\rVert_{2}\leq\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}-\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\mathbf{R}\rVert_{2}\leq\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}-\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda\leq\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda\leq\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\leq 1/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\leq 1/L</annotation></semantics></math>,
the event
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert{\bm{e}}_{a}-{\bm{e}}_{b}\rVert_{2}&lt;\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert{\bm{e}}_{a}-{\bm{e}}_{b}\rVert_{2}&lt;\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>
is implied by
<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\mathbf{S}\rVert_{2}&lt;\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}-\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝐒</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\mathbf{S}\rVert_{2}&lt;\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}-\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, for <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda\leq\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda\leq\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(D_{p}&lt;\lambda)=\Pr\!\bigl(\lVert{\bm{e}}_{a}-{\bm{e}}_{b}\rVert_{2}&lt;\lambda\bigr)\;\geq\;\Pr\!\bigl(\lVert\mathbf{S}\rVert_{2}&lt;\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}-\lambda\bigr)\;\geq\;1-\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{(\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}-\lambda)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.090em;" width="1.090em"><mi>Pr</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.090em;" width="1.090em"><mi>Pr</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝐒</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(D_{p}&lt;\lambda)=\Pr\!\bigl(\lVert{\bm{e}}_{a}-{\bm{e}}_{b}\rVert_{2}&lt;\lambda\bigr)\;\geq\;\Pr\!\bigl(\lVert\mathbf{S}\rVert_{2}&lt;\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}-\lambda\bigr)\;\geq\;1-\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{(\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}-\lambda)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Deterministic regime.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition A.2 (Embedding Wasserstein Bound). ‣ A.1 Lemma &amp; Propositions ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theoretical Analysis ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> ensures <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{p}\leq\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{p}\leq\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math> almost surely,
so <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(D_{p}&lt;\lambda)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(D_{p}&lt;\lambda)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq\Delta_{{\mathcal{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Combining the two regimes completes the proof.
∎</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Proof for Theorem 2</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2 (Prediction‑Agreement Probability). ‣ 4 Theoretical Analysis ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\bm{z}}}-{\bm{z}}=\bm{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝜹</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\bm{z}}}-{\bm{z}}=\bm{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the Fréchet expansion of <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}+{\bm{u}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{f}}_{{\bm{w}}+{\bm{u}}}</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\delta}=\sum_{k=1}^{L}\Bigl(\tfrac{\partial{\bm{f}}}{\partial{\bm{W}}_{k}}\Bigr){\bm{U}}_{k}\;+\;\mathbf{R},\qquad\text{with }\;\lVert\mathbf{R}\rVert_{2}=O(\eta^{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜹</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝒇</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑼</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>with </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\delta}=\sum_{k=1}^{L}\Bigl(\tfrac{\partial{\bm{f}}}{\partial{\bm{W}}_{k}}\Bigr){\bm{U}}_{k}\;+\;\mathbf{R},\qquad\text{with }\;\lVert\mathbf{R}\rVert_{2}=O(\eta^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Because <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\leq 1/L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\leq 1/L</annotation></semantics></math> (Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1 (MPGNN Perturbation Bound ( , )). ‣ A.1 Lemma &amp; Propositions ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theoretical Analysis ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>), the remainder <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is of smaller order than
<math id="A1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and can be absorbed into the constant implicit in the tail bound below, so we focus on the linear term</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}=\sum_{k=1}^{L}\Bigl(\tfrac{\partial{\bm{f}}}{\partial{\bm{W}}_{k}}\Bigr){\bm{U}}_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝒇</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑼</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}=\sum_{k=1}^{L}\Bigl(\tfrac{\partial{\bm{f}}}{\partial{\bm{W}}_{k}}\Bigr){\bm{U}}_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">Each coordinate <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is a mean‑zero sub‑Gaussian random variable with proxy variance
at most <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in the Notation paragraph.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Misclassification.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The predicted class changes from <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> to some <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}\neq c^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}\neq c^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> only if</p>
<table id="A1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{c^{\ast}}\leq-\tfrac{\gamma}{2}\quad\text{or}\quad S_{j}\geq\tfrac{\gamma}{2}\ \text{for some }j\neq c^{\ast}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>or</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>for some </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{c^{\ast}}\leq-\tfrac{\gamma}{2}\quad\text{or}\quad S_{j}\geq\tfrac{\gamma}{2}\ \text{for some }j\neq c^{\ast}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tail probabilities.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a sub‑Gaussian variable <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with proxy variance <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pr(X\geq t)\leq\exp(-t^{2}/(2\sigma^{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(X\geq t)\leq\exp(-t^{2}/(2\sigma^{2}))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(X\leq-t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(X\leq-t)</annotation></semantics></math> obeys the same bound.
Setting <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\gamma/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\gamma/2</annotation></semantics></math> yields</p>
<table id="A1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr\!\bigl(S_{c^{\ast}}\leq-\tfrac{\gamma}{2}\bigr)\leq\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{\gamma^{2}}{8\sigma^{2}}\Bigr),\qquad\Pr\!\bigl(S_{j}\geq\tfrac{\gamma}{2}\bigr)\leq\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{\gamma^{2}}{8\sigma^{2}}\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.090em;" width="1.090em"><mi>Pr</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.090em;" width="1.090em"><mi>Pr</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\!\bigl(S_{c^{\ast}}\leq-\tfrac{\gamma}{2}\bigr)\leq\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{\gamma^{2}}{8\sigma^{2}}\Bigr),\qquad\Pr\!\bigl(S_{j}\geq\tfrac{\gamma}{2}\bigr)\leq\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{\gamma^{2}}{8\sigma^{2}}\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Union bound.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Applying the union bound over the <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C-1)</annotation></semantics></math> classes <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq c^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq c^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> and the single negative‑tail event for <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(\tilde{c}\neq c^{\ast})\leq(C-1)\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{\gamma^{2}}{8\sigma^{2}}\Bigr)+\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{\gamma^{2}}{8\sigma^{2}}\Bigr)=C\,\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{\gamma^{2}}{8\sigma^{2}}\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(\tilde{c}\neq c^{\ast})\leq(C-1)\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{\gamma^{2}}{8\sigma^{2}}\Bigr)+\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{\gamma^{2}}{8\sigma^{2}}\Bigr)=C\,\exp\!\Bigl(-\tfrac{\gamma^{2}}{8\sigma^{2}}\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A slightly sharper bound, keeping only the <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C-1)</annotation></semantics></math> positive‑tail events, is
<math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pr(\tilde{c}\neq c^{\ast})\leq(C-1)\exp(-\gamma^{2}/(8\sigma^{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>8</mn><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(\tilde{c}\neq c^{\ast})\leq(C-1)\exp(-\gamma^{2}/(8\sigma^{2}))</annotation></semantics></math>;
subtracting from one gives the stated inequality.
∎</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Supplementary Technical Details</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Assumptions for Model Extraction Attacks &amp; Defense</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our signature generation method is based on a widely accepted assumption that the decision boundary of an independently trained model is inherently unique <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Waheed et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. These works provide both theoretical justification and empirical observations, demonstrating that this assumption holds broadly. Consequently, the design objective of our signature is to extract nodes that most effectively represent the boundary information as our signature.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption B.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Decision Boundary Uniqueness)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Independently trained models, even when trained on the same dataset and architecture, possess unique output distributions and decision boundaries. Therefore, it is feasible to extract a signature that captures these boundaries and can serve as a unique identifier for model ownership verification.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we follow the assumption that attackers typically attempt to query information located at the decision boundary, which is widely adopted in other works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brendel et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Experimental Evaluations ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we further validate that if an attacker aims to more effectively replicate the functionality of the target model, they must query information near the decision boundary to achieve improved performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption B.2</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Boundary Query Preference)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adversarial model extraction attacks tend to query inputs near the decision boundary to maximize information gain, while benign users predominantly operate in high-confidence regions. Consequently, restricting access to boundary information can significantly degrade the effectiveness of surrogate models trained by attackers.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To thoroughly analyze the effectiveness of our model, we require a more detailed definition of the attacker’s capabilities. Specifically, an attacker cannot successfully replicate the behavior of a target model without accessing a sufficient amount of query-response data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tramèr et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papernot et al., 2017</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption B.3</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Minimum Effectiveness Query)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To train an effective surrogate model, an attacker needs sufficient query-response pairs from the target model. The surrogate’s performance is limited by a minimum query budget. Below this threshold, the surrogate cannot reliably approximate the target model.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>ARUC Computation for Ownership Verification</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify ownership verification performance, we employ the <em id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Area under the Robustness-Uniqueness Curve</em> (ARUC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, which jointly considers the distinguishability of surrogate and independent models with respect to the signature node set <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}_{\text{sig}}\subseteq{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mtext>sig</mtext></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}_{\text{sig}}\subseteq{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The evaluation proceeds in three steps: (1) computing a level-specific matching score <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(f_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(f_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> between a suspicious model <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and the target model <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, (2) constructing robustness and uniqueness curves over a range of decision thresholds <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, and (3) aggregating the two curves into a single ARUC value. Formally, let <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the sets of surrogate (positive) and independent (negative) models, respectively. The robustness curve <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> and uniqueness curve <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R(\tau)=\frac{1}{|F^{+}|}\sum_{f_{Q}\in F^{+}}\mathbf{1}[M(f_{Q})\bowtie\tau],\quad U(\tau)=\frac{1}{|F^{-}|}\sum_{f_{Q}\in F^{-}}\mathbf{1}[M(f_{Q})\bowtie^{\prime}\tau]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⋈</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mo>⋈</mo><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\tau)=\frac{1}{|F^{+}|}\sum_{f_{Q}\in F^{+}}\mathbf{1}[M(f_{Q})\bowtie\tau],\quad U(\tau)=\frac{1}{|F^{-}|}\sum_{f_{Q}\in F^{-}}\mathbf{1}[M(f_{Q})\bowtie^{\prime}\tau],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bowtie" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bowtie</annotation></semantics></math> is a thresholding operator determined by the definition of the matching score at each output level, and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bowtie^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⋈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bowtie^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes its logical complement. Specifically, at the <em id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">embedding level</em>, the matching score is defined as the Wasserstein distance between the signature embeddings of the suspicious and target models. Since surrogate models are expected to lie closer in embedding space, the operator is set to <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, at the <em id="A2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">label level</em>, the matching score measures the prediction accuracy on ownership indicators, where higher values reflect stronger alignment; hence, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bowtie" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bowtie</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bowtie" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bowtie</annotation></semantics></math> is a thresholding operator determined by the definition of the matching score at each output level, and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bowtie^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⋈</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bowtie^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes its logical complement. Specifically, at the <em id="A2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">embedding level</em>, the matching score is defined as the Wasserstein distance between the signature embeddings of the suspicious and target models. Since surrogate models are expected to lie closer in embedding space, the operator is set to <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, at the <em id="A2.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">label level</em>, the matching score measures the prediction accuracy on ownership indicators, where higher values reflect stronger alignment; hence, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bowtie" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bowtie</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>. Due to the nature of model extraction attacks, where surrogate models often exhibit moderate but not perfect alignment with the target, we normalize all matching scores across models before computing ARUC to ensure fair and comparable evaluation. To summarize the trade-off between robustness and uniqueness across thresholds, we compute the ARUC as:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{ARUC}=\frac{1}{r}\sum_{\tau^{\prime}=1}^{r}\min\left\{R\left(\frac{\tau^{\prime}}{r}\right),U\left(\frac{\tau^{\prime}}{r}\right)\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>ARUC</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ARUC}=\frac{1}{r}\sum_{\tau^{\prime}=1}^{r}\min\left\{R\left(\frac{\tau^{\prime}}{r}\right),U\left(\frac{\tau^{\prime}}{r}\right)\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of discrete threshold points sampled uniformly from the interval <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. A higher ARUC score indicates that the signature set enables strong alignment with surrogate models while effectively rejecting independent ones, thus ensuring reliable ownership verification. To summarize the trade-off between robustness and uniqueness across thresholds, we compute the ARUC as:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{ARUC}=\frac{1}{r}\sum_{\tau^{\prime}=1}^{r}\min\left\{R\left(\frac{\tau^{\prime}}{r}\right),U\left(\frac{\tau^{\prime}}{r}\right)\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>ARUC</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ARUC}=\frac{1}{r}\sum_{\tau^{\prime}=1}^{r}\min\left\{R\left(\frac{\tau^{\prime}}{r}\right),U\left(\frac{\tau^{\prime}}{r}\right)\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of discrete threshold points sampled uniformly from the interval <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. A higher ARUC score indicates that the signature set enables strong alignment with surrogate models while effectively rejecting independent ones, thus ensuring reliable ownership verification.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>AUC Computation for Ownership Verification</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we adopt the <span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve</span> (AUC) to evaluate the effectiveness of ownership verification. AUC quantifies the probability that a randomly selected surrogate model (i.e., a model suspected of being an unauthorized copy) yields a higher verification score than a randomly selected independent model. This provides an intuitive measure of how well the verification scores can distinguish surrogate models from independently trained ones. Formally, let <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the outputs of suspicious surrogate models (positive samples), and <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{z}_{j}^{-}\}_{j=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{z}_{j}^{-}\}_{j=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the outputs of independently trained models (negative samples). For each output <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a score function <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\mathbf{z})=\text{score}(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{z}_{\text{target}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>score</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\mathbf{z})=\text{score}(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{z}_{\text{target}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which measures the agreement between <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> and the protected target model’s output. The AUC is computed as:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{AUC}=\frac{1}{nm}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}\mathbb{1}\left[s(\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+})&gt;s(\mathbf{z}_{j}^{-})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>AUC</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AUC}=\frac{1}{nm}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}\mathbb{1}\left[s(\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+})&gt;s(\mathbf{z}_{j}^{-})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This corresponds to the Mann–Whitney U statistic, a standard formulation of AUC that captures the degree of separation between the score distributions of surrogate and independent models without requiring a fixed threshold. To account for tie cases where <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+})=s(\mathbf{z}_{j}^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+})=s(\mathbf{z}_{j}^{-})</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply averaging as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AUC}=\frac{1}{nm}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}\left[\mathbb{1}(s(\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+})&gt;s(\mathbf{z}_{j}^{-}))+\frac{1}{2}\cdot\mathbb{1}(s(\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+})=s(\mathbf{z}_{j}^{-}))\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>AUC</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>𝟙</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AUC}=\frac{1}{nm}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}\left[\mathbb{1}(s(\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+})&gt;s(\mathbf{z}_{j}^{-}))+\frac{1}{2}\cdot\mathbb{1}(s(\mathbf{z}_{i}^{+})=s(\mathbf{z}_{j}^{-}))\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The definition of the score function <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the output level. At the <span id="A2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">embedding level</span>, <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the node embeddings of signature nodes, and the score <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\mathbf{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\mathbf{z})</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated as the negative Wasserstein distance to the target model’s embedding distribution. A higher score thus indicates stronger alignment with the target model. At the <span id="A2.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">label level</span>, <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the predicted class labels over the signature nodes, and the score <math id="A2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\mathbf{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\mathbf{z})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the classification accuracy with respect to the target model’s predictions. In this case, a higher score reflects greater consistency in decision-making. By computing AUC independently at both the embedding and label levels, we obtain a unified yet adaptable metric to quantify how effectively surrogate models can be distinguished from non-infringing models across different output representations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Time Complexity of Signature Generation</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The overall time complexity of signature generation is:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n\cdot B\cdot d+n\cdot k)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n\cdot B\cdot d+n\cdot k),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of nodes, <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of boundary nodes, <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding dimension, and <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the average node degree. The first term comes from computing the margin and thickness scores relative to boundary nodes, while the second term reflects the cost of evaluating local label heterogeneity. Given that <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\ll n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\ll n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is small for sparse graphs, the procedure scales efficiently with graph size.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Supplementary Experimental Settings</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Experimental Data Statistics</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ C.1 Experimental Data Statistics ‣ Appendix C Supplementary Experimental Settings ‣ CITED: A Decision Boundary-Aware Signature for GNNs Towards Model Extraction Defense" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> summarizes the statistics of the real-world graph datasets used in our experiments. These datasets are widely adopted in graph neural network (GNN) research, particularly for evaluating performance on node classification tasks. They cover a diverse range of domains, including citation networks (Cora, CiteSeer, PubMed), co-purchase graphs (Amazon-Photo, Amazon-Computers), and co-authorship networks (Coauthor-CS, Coauthor-Physics). All datasets represent realistic graph structures and have been extensively benchmarked in the literature.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A3.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="A3.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Statistics of the adopted real-world graph datasets.</span></figcaption>
<table id="A3.T4.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A3.T4.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T4.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">#Nodes</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">#Edges</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">#Attributes</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">#Classes</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T4.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T4.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Cora</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">2,708</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">5,429</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1,433</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">7</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T4.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T4.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">CiteSeer</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">3,327</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">4,723</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">3,703</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">6</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T4.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T4.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">PubMed</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">19,717</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">88,648</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">500</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">3</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T4.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T4.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Amazon-Photo</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">13,752</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">491,722</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">767</td>
<td id="A3.T4.5.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">10</td></tr>
